@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# OpenCode Customized Features
+
+This document summarizes the main features added or changed in this repository relative to upstream OpenCode. It helps maintainers and contributors understand what these private changes address, how they behave, and where their implementations live. This is not a general description of upstream OpenCode; refer to the current repository code for the exact behavior.
+
+OpenCode's default agent workflow works best when a model can reliably follow instructions, emit structured tool calls, and report useful context limits. Smaller and locally hosted models, from a few billion to tens of billions of parameters, can be less consistent: they may express tool calls as plain text, produce malformed arguments, repeat failing calls, or provide incomplete context-limit information. These repository-specific changes aim to make OpenCode's agent workflows more reliable with that range of models by improving tool-call recovery, context budgeting, and failure handling, while preserving the existing behavior for models that already work well with the standard flow.
+
+## 1. Context Budgeting and Automatic Compaction
+
+These changes aim to use a consistent context-budget calculation, make compaction triggers easier to diagnose, and prevent inaccurate estimates from sending a session into a repeated-compaction loop.
+
+* **Scale the reserve to the context window.** When a separate input limit is available, the default reserve is 15% of the window, bounded between 2,048 and 20,000 tokens. The `compaction.reserved` config value takes precedence. This avoids reserving too much for smaller windows or letting the reserve grow without bound for larger ones.
+* **Use one budget calculation.** `budget()` returns `context`, `reserve`, and `usable`. Proactive compaction, request output limits, headroom checks, and telemetry all use the same budget result.
+* **Log compaction decisions.** Proactive compaction logs at step-finish and loop-entry record the trigger, window, token count, reserve, usable budget, and decision. When a provider reports context overflow, the estimated size of the failed request and the budget are also logged to help diagnose the trigger.
+
+Main code: [`overflow.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/overflow.ts), [`processor.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/processor.ts), [`compaction.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/compaction.ts), and [`prompt.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts).
+
+## 2. Pre-Request Budgeting, Output Limits, and File-Read Protection
+
+This work shares the budget used by automatic compaction but does not trigger compaction directly. It aims to catch budget risks before sending a request or loading file contents into the context.
+
+* **Estimate the next request size and improve the estimate.** Request preparation estimates the token cost of the system prompt, message history, and tool schemas. This estimate was first used to limit model output based on the remaining context, and was later also exposed through telemetry. More recent changes improve the estimator: when no exact tokenizer is available, digits and Chinese text use more suitable heuristics, and request, Read, and compaction paths share a consistent estimation entry point.
+* **Limit model output.** When the usable budget is known, the estimated request input is used to lower `maxOutputTokens`. This avoids allowing a long output when the input is already close to the context limit. Once the estimate reaches the budget, the output limit is left unchanged: the estimate usually runs above the provider's count, and proactive compaction compares the provider-reported usage, so flooring output at one token would stall the session below the compaction threshold. A request that really exceeds the window is rejected by the provider and handled by reactive compaction.
+* **Block oversized Read operations.** The Read tool uses headroom recorded for the most recent request to reject file reads that could consume too much remaining context, and prompts the model to read a smaller range.
+* **Expose budget information to external routers.** When a meaningful budget is available, headers such as `x-opencode-context-*` and `x-opencode-baseline-*` carry the window, reserve, and input estimate to a fronting router or proxy for routing decisions.
+
+Token estimation has also been made more consistent. Earlier changes added format-specific densities for CSV, JSON, logs, and similar content. More recent changes added a tiered approach: prefer an exact tokenizer, then use digit- and Chinese-aware heuristics when one is unavailable. Read, compaction, request budgeting, and overflow diagnostics reuse the same estimation entry points where possible, so the same content is less likely to be estimated on different scales in different parts of the codebase.
+
+Main code: [`request.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/llm/request.ts), [`read.ts`](packages/opencode/src/tool/read.ts), and [`token.ts`](packages/core/src/util/token.ts).
+
+## 3. Small-Model and Non-Standard Tool-Call Compatibility
+
+These changes address practical compatibility problems: some local or smaller models do not emit tool calls in the expected format. They may write calls as ordinary text, produce invalid JSON, or repeatedly invoke a failing tool.
+
+* **Apply behavior by model tier.** Models can be classified as `minimal`, `default`, or `vendor`; the tier can be configured or inferred from the provider family and model ID. Extra compatibility handling mainly targets the minimal tier, aiming to leave the default behavior of mainstream vendor models unchanged.
+* **Convert text-form tool calls into actual calls.** For models that do not support native tool calls or are classified as minimal, the runtime recognizes selected `<tool_call>` formats, JSON code fences, and clearly shaped JSON objects. When the tool name is valid, it converts the text into a tool-call event.
+* **Repair common tool-argument JSON errors.** The runtime attempts to fix smart quotes, single quotes, Python-style booleans and nulls, trailing commas, and unbalanced brackets. A repaired result is executed only if it passes the tool schema validation.
+* **Break repeated failing tool calls.** When the minimal tier detects a structural doom loop, it temporarily removes the relevant tool from subsequent requests to stop the model from repeating the same failing call.
+* **Actually disable tools on the final step.** When the maximum step count is reached, callable tools are removed from the request instead of relying only on prompt text telling the model not to call them.
+* **Remove think tags leaked into regular text.** For the minimal tier, completed ordinary text has `<think>...</think>` content removed.
+* **Improve plugin runtime resilience.** Plugins load in lexicographic order, making filename prefixes useful for controlling load order. Errors from individual plugins in non-blocking hooks are logged and isolated instead of preventing later plugins from running. Hooks with blocking semantics, such as permission checks and pre-tool execution, still fail fast.
+
+Main code: [`tier.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/tier.ts), [`llm.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/llm.ts), [`repair.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/llm/repair.ts), [`textcall.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/llm/textcall.ts), [`doom-loop.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/doom-loop.ts), and [`plugin/index.ts`](packages/opencode/src/plugin/index.ts).
+
+## 4. Session and MCP APIs
+
+* **Session search.** Adds the ability to search session titles and message contents by text, with filters for directory, root sessions, archived sessions, and result count. When searching subagent content, matches can be attributed to the root session the user would actually open.
+* **MCP tool discovery.** Adds an endpoint for listing the tools exposed by a given MCP server, so callers can discover its available tools.
+
+Main code: [`search.ts`](packages/opencode/src/session/search.ts), [`session handlers`](packages/opencode/src/server/routes/instance/httpapi/handlers/session.ts), and [`MCP handlers`](packages/opencode/src/server/routes/instance/httpapi/handlers/mcp.ts).
+
+## 5. Fork-Specific Environment Adaptations
+
+There are also changes tailored to this repository's environment, such as CI runner and proxy configuration updates, enabling or disabling selected GitHub workflows, and changing the OAuth display name to Intel SuperClaw. These are fork-specific engineering and branding adaptations, not changes to OpenCode's core agent behavior.
+
+Config loading also tolerates a read-only `.opencode` or config directory (for example a read-only bind mount): when it cannot write the generated `.gitignore` because the filesystem is read-only (`EROFS`), it skips the file instead of exiting. Main code: [`config.ts`](packages/opencode/src/config/config.ts) `ensureGitignore`.
+
+## Notes for Readers
+
+This document is an overview of the repository's private changes. When investigating specific behavior, refer to the linked implementations and tests. Attempts that were later reverted in the commit history should not be treated as descriptions of current functionality.
